## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical understanding of lightweight, policy-driven OTCA telemetry for multi-tenant apps, emphasizing real-time decisioning, regulatory retention, and mobility across regions.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant residency and retention controls
- Ingestion-time redaction and field dropping
- Lightweight per-tenant policy engine with dynamic updates
- Edge-to-streaming architecture: edge gateway, regional Kafka, Parquet on S3 with tenant/date partitions
- Privacy, QoS, and policy-change verification

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo policy evaluator
function evaluate(event, policy) {
  if (policy.drop) return null;
  if (policy.redactUserId) event.user_id_hash = null;
  return event;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test policy updates without downtime?
- How would you monitor for privacy violations in production?

## Why This Is Asked
OTCA cross-tenant logging scenario focusing on verifiable data erasure and auditable provenance, not just privacy.

## Key Concepts
- OTCA residency enforcement and tenant scoping
- Deletion workflows, deletion certificates, and verifiable erasure
- Edge DP masking of sensitive fields before transport
- Immutable audit ledger with cryptographic proofs
- Per-tenant retention policies and tombstoning

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: handleDeletion(tenantId, rideId)
function handleDeletion(tenantId, rideId){
  regionStore.erase(tenantId, rideId);
  const cert = createDeletionCert(tenantId, rideId, hash(regionStore.snapshot()));
  auditLog.append(cert);
  centralLedger.commit(cert);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test audit-log immutability under clock skew and partial failures?
- How would you handle cross-region deletion consistency during network partitions?

